A||||10||rodmcneill||Panel warpage?||||5||
Z||000000||rodmcneill||08-24-2001||11:22 PM||||What parts of the Fiero body are likely to warp with age? I didn't realise this was a problem with Fiero's untill I saw a picture of a sagging wing.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fformula88||08-24-2001||11:30 PM||||I am not sure the wing is a common problem, but interior pieces are!<P>The center console aroud the shifter will have the vinyl warp and pull away from the top plate.  Same with the armrest around the bottome edge, the glove box, and the bod around the radio/climate control.<P>Keeping the interior cool and out of the sun will help this a lot!||||reg||1
Z||000002||MinnGreenGT||08-25-2001||01:02 AM||||I have what some would call the "sagging wing"  BUT I also have a theory on that.  I read in a recent thread that the two wing posts (left & right) are different, only slightly, but still different.  My car has been through a few body shops- at least one before I owned it.  It is certainly possible that the two were switched and this change is causing a downward pressure to be put on the middle of the wing.  I may go out and switch the posts just for awhile to see if it makes a differenc.  Other than that, I am unaware of any sagging that would occur.  The most common problem with older Fieros (my '84) is the fiber in the panels coming through the paint making a rough texture.  Don't really know how else to describe it.  Some extreme form of oxidization maybe?<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg"> <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
Z||000003||rodmcneill||08-25-2001||01:31 AM||||I have seen pictures of some Fiero's that seem to have some sagging where the nose meets the hood, right by the emblem.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfixit58||08-25-2001||07:57 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rodmcneill:<BR><B>I have seen pictures of some Fiero's that seem to have some sagging where the nose meets the hood, right by the emblem.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I've got one like that in my back yard, now. A point of interest: I've seen several cars (including my own) where the panels don't seem to fit quite right. The front, lower quarter on the passenger side (opposite of the factory cool air intake) is a good example. You can see a gap between the frame and the panel. I've noticed this on several other including some very low mileage unrestored cars. I looks like the lower attachment point was installed too high which caused the panel to bow in the middle.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||9
Z||000005||Galen||08-25-2001||09:16 AM||||my nose sags.. (the one on my 88).. if anyone wants it for scientific study, I'll gladly take a aero fascia in place of it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/sig.gif">  Ray -    <A HREF="mailto:galen@ezrpg.com">galen@ezrpg.com</A>    - <A HREF="http://www.fierolinks.f2s.com/" TARGET=_blank>www.FieroLinks.f2s.com</A><BR>AIM: GalenRPG - ICQ: 98668558||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cheever3000||08-25-2001||09:25 AM||||Galen, it's not your <I>car</I> that needs scientific study.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||08-25-2001||12:28 PM||||My Formula has the sagging problem in the front facia.<P>Maybe they need bras?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Seriously, is there any way to fix it other than replacing it?||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||08-25-2001||01:45 PM||||The nose sag is because there's nothing under it. I've not had time to fool with a fix. I imagine a piece of styrofoam could be cut to fit there and fix the problem. It might not be perfect but it should eliminate the obvious sagging.<P>Other than that the rest of the panels are pretty stable.<P>I've heard of wing sag before. Usually with aftermarket wings. If this is an OE wing, there may have been more than one source used by GM and one of them may not be as good as the other(s). There's probably nothing you can do about it sagging except replace it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Mach10||08-25-2001||03:42 PM||||Solution:<BR>I've seen this before, but can't remember where. I will not take credit for it:<P>Take the wing off and brace it against a flat, true peice of wood. Heat the Mo'Fo up to soften it a little with a paint stripping gun, and lash it tight against the brace. Next, drill a few small holes in it at 12" intervals in a hard to see area. Grab a can of Mono Expando-Foam, (maximum expansion stuff) and start filling the wing. Don't stop until it oozes out. Let it cure, cut the excess off, drill a little into it, and lay some body-filler into it, Sand flush, and spray-bomb the affected areas. This is why you do it in the inconspicuous area, like the bottom. This way you don't have to repaint the whole thing.||||reg||6
Z||000010||artherd||08-25-2001||07:40 PM||||the plastic sections of the fiero (front and rear bumpers, door skins...) are just crap for this kind of thing.  Eventually they will all sag, this sutff moves, it is not stable.<P>Only way to fix it is replacement, only to fix it perminantly is to use a different material. :( :(<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
